<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">The object under examination</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-baseline">What RH actually says</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="wrap narrow">
    <div class="frame">
      <p>The completed zeta function may be written</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        \xi(s)=\frac12s(s-1)\pi^{-s/2}\Gamma\!\left(\frac{s}{2}\right)\zeta(s),
        \qquad
        \Xi(t)=\xi\!\left(\frac12+it\right).
      \]</p>
      <p>The functional equation is \(\xi(s)=\xi(1-s)\). The Riemann Hypothesis says that every
        non-trivial zero \(\rho\) satisfies \(\Re\rho=\tfrac12\). It is a universal location statement:
        not that many zeros lie on the line, not that every tested zero does, and not that a finite matrix is
        positive, but that <em>every</em> non-trivial zero lies there. No calculation in the supplied archive
        discharges that universal quantifier.</p>
      <p>The diary explores many equivalent or potentially related languages: Laguerre inequalities, Hankel
        determinants, Jacobi matrices, Weil quadratic forms, Loewner matrices, Pick functions, screw
        functions, moment sequences and finite Galerkin operators. These translations are useful because a
        hard location statement may become a positivity statement with visible structure. Translation is not
        completion. If the new positivity condition is itself equivalent to RH, restating it does not make it
        easier unless some independent theorem controls the required sign.</p>
      <p>That distinction governs this dossier. A reformulation can be exact and illuminating while making no
        progress towards proof. A finite computation can be spectacularly precise while remaining finite.
        A conditional formula can reveal what RH would organise while being unavailable as an unconditional
        premise. Each of those is worth recording, but none may borrow the evidential grade of another.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

