<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">One result that closes internally</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-log-convexity">Strict log-convexity on the real axis</h2>
    <p>The cleanest surviving argument begins with the positive-kernel representation, up to an irrelevant
      positive normalisation,</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      M(r)=\xi\!\left(\frac12+r\right)
      =C\int_0^\infty\Phi(u)\cosh(ru)\,du,
      \qquad C>0,\quad\Phi(u)>0.
    \]</p>
    <p>Normalise the integrand to a probability measure
      \(d\mu_r=\Phi(u)\cosh(ru)\,du/M(r)\). Differentiation gives</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      (\log M)''(r)=
      \mathbb E_r[u^2]
      -\bigl(\mathbb E_r[u\tanh(ru)]\bigr)^2.
    \]</p>
    <p>Cauchy&ndash;Schwarz bounds the squared second term by
      \(\mathbb E_r[u^2]\mathbb E_r[\tanh^2(ru)]\), and the latter multiplier is strictly below one for
      finite \(r>0\). At \(r=0\), the odd derivative vanishes and the second moment is positive. Reflection
      through the functional equation covers the other half of the real axis. Therefore</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      (\log\xi)''(\sigma)>0\qquad(\sigma\in\mathbb R).
    \]</p>
    <p>This is an internally reproducible result. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> The archive calls it new;
      this dossier does not. Establishing novelty requires a systematic literature comparison beyond the
      supplied notes. The proof also stays on the real axis, where \(\cosh\) is positive. Replacing it with
      the oscillatory \(\cos(tu)\) on the critical line destroys the positive probability measure, so the
      same Cauchy&ndash;Schwarz step does not prove RH.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame card">
      <p class="kicker">Exact calculations retained</p>
      <h2 id="t-rh-balances">Balance integrals</h2>
      <p>Several cancellations in the diary reduce to elementary Gamma- or trigonometric-integral
        identities:</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        \int_0^\infty(2u^2-1)e^{-u^2}\,du=0,
      \]</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        \int_0^\infty(2u^2-1)e^{-u^2}\log u\,du=\frac{\sqrt\pi}{2},
      \]</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        \int_0^\infty\frac{3u^2-1}{(1+u^2)^3}\,du=0,
        \quad
        \int_0^\infty\frac{3u^2-1}{(1+u^2)^3}\log u\,du=\frac\pi4.
      \]</p>
      <p>These equalities are exact. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> Their zero leading terms explain why a
        logarithmic correction can control a smooth approximation. They do not prove that a discrete sum over
        all zeros has the same sign. That requires an explicit remainder estimate strong enough for every
        parameter value.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <p class="kicker">Conditional algebra retained</p>
      <h2 id="t-rh-conditional-density">The corrected \(F\) and its density</h2>
      <p>Under RH, pairing zeros with ordinates \(\gamma_k>0\) yields</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        F(x)=2\sum_k\frac{\gamma_k^2-x}{(x+\gamma_k^2)^2}.
      \]</p>
      <p>The archive originally replaced this with \(2\sum(x+\gamma_k^2)^{-1}\), then correctly retracted
        that substitution. Inverting each corrected term gives</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        \rho(t)=2\sum_k(2\gamma_k^2t-1)e^{-\gamma_k^2t},
        \qquad
        F(x)=\int_0^\infty e^{-xt}\rho(t)\,dt,
      \]</p>
      <p>where interchange of sum and integral must be justified. The inversion is exact under its
        assumptions. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span> Positivity of \(\rho\) is a different claim; the supplied
        proof of it fails for small \(t\), as the correction ledger explains.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">What the computations do show</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-cooperative-rescue">Cooperative rescue is a pattern, not yet a theorem</h2>
    <p>The archive’s most memorable phrase is <strong>cooperative rescue</strong>. In selected finite Weil or
      Galerkin matrices, an archimedean contribution and individual prime contributions can each be indefinite,
      while their computed sum is positive. Perturbing frequencies or amplitudes may destroy that positivity.
      This is an informative observation about exact arithmetic coordination in those experiments.</p>
    <p>Its present evidential grade is numerical. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span> The tested cutoffs, basis
      sizes and precision do not establish positivity for every admissible test function. Nor does one
      indefinite decomposition imply that nature uses a physical rescue mechanism. A quadratic form may have
      many decompositions; the sign of separate summands is not invariant unless the decomposition itself is
      canonically justified.</p>
    <p>The right use of these experiments is diagnostic. They identify where error budgets must be explicit,
      where a finite truncation sits inside an inconclusive band, and where a proof cannot simply estimate each
      prime contribution independently. The wrong use would be to name a tiny positive eigenvalue as evidence
      for a universal theorem, or to translate matrix fragility into cosmological fine tuning without a map
      between the two systems.</p>
    <p>Likewise, finite checks of Laguerre inequalities, Hankel determinants, Turán gaps or zero approximants
      demonstrate that an implementation can reproduce expected local behaviour. They do not establish the
      all-orders or all-heights condition. Precision changes the quality of a finite statement; it does not
      change its quantifier.</p>
  </div>
</section>

